#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111504} :activity/id #uuid "d5d46167-9ce6-5517-bbfb-42877effd770" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383 :topic/id #uuid "44b10216-6553-5a54-ba7f-65d318a0317e" :topic/math-academy-id 1415} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111913 :tutorial/content "Loosely speaking, a **rational equation** is an equation containing rational expressions.\n\nWhen a rational equation contains only one fractional term, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nwe can multiply both sides of the equation by the denominator to remove the fraction. This leaves us with a linear equation, which we can then solve using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nBefore we proceed, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because if $x=0$ then the rational term would be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we multiply both sides by the denominator ${\\color{blue} x}$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x} &= 1 \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{2}{x} &= x \\cdot 1 \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{2}{x} &= x \\cdot 1 \\\\\n2 &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a963a90e-094e-50e9-b621-0f4c926aa056" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8778 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e0e87557-b986-5f0e-a2c4-35f24864dae5" :step/math-academy-id 147050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110603 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110326 :question/id #uuid "deda4937-0332-5584-b6a1-fa840e750be1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14138"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69089475-1b80-5230-941c-433dd6cd564c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111329}] :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing a Variable in the Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "8981ae8b-4bb4-50e4-95f8-58cf76eee551" :step/math-academy-id 147051 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110604 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110327 :question/id #uuid "9640178a-cada-5fd6-8622-9bef515ed443" :question/math-academy-id "e-14141"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb34628f-b5ab-5ce7-8d92-576f5adaf54d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111222}] :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing Variables in the Numerator and Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "9e05244a-f286-5f45-80e1-ecf030e56f7a" :step/math-academy-id 147055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111507}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110605 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110328 :question/id #uuid "d4adcae2-06f8-5d3d-ac48-1aca6c8a0c29" :question/math-academy-id "e-14144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa33473c-6431-59c9-a96c-1e99fa959058" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111221}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Leading Coefficient in the Denominator Is Not One"} :step/id #uuid "86f8f5a8-177b-5fa6-908b-d2f52901954f" :step/math-academy-id 147058}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Fractional Term" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110811}]}
